 "statement": "A NEW DIRECTLY-MEASURED OBSERVABLE -- the mass-weighted acceleration distribution of the dark sector, dM/dg, computed ring by ring from the committed rotation-curve samples (SPARC 361 shells from 35 galaxies / 641 rings, HI 26 deep-end mass elements from 26 LITTLE THINGS dwarfs, MIGHTEE 80 g_obs rings [no committed radii -> shape-only, equal-weight]).  V1 (power law, g < 0.3 a0): the composite deep dM/dg is NOT a single g^-2 law on any pooled estimator: pooled log-log slope of dM/dg vs g over g < 0.3 a0 = +0.08 +- 0.61 (z = +3.41 vs -2) and, with the noise-dominated shells (Delta g/g < 0.02) removed, +0.05 +- 0.58 (z = +3.53); binned-median slope +0.88 (composite RISING, not falling), the mass-weighted survival index p = +3.47 +- 0.20 (z = +7.28); PER-HALO the story differs: the median per-galaxy deep index is -1.47 (consistent with -2) but with per-galaxy scatter std 15.8 (n = 16 galaxies, 3-8 deep shells each) -- too noisy to pin, and consistent with the KINEMATIC IDENTITY |dM/dg| = v_flat^4/(G g^2) that ANY near-flat rotation curve satisfies (so per-halo agreement is not independent evidence for the phantom) -- the measured index vs the H055 phantom prediction -2: NOT CONSISTENT (SPARC z = +3.41, pool z = +7.28, HI z = +3.43).  V2 (Lomax shape, FULL distribution): the full-range Lomax KS p = 0.00 (SPARC), 0.00 (pooled), 0.23 (HI, n=26, weak), 0.00 (MIGHTEE eq-weight); mass-fraction chi2 1.25/8 (p_mc 0.01) and 3.57/8 (p_mc 0.00); tail-only (g < 0.3 a0, conditional) KS p = 0.00 (SPARC) / 0.00 (pooled) -- the full shape DEPARTS; tail-only KS p = 0.00 / 0.00 (tail also departs); caveats: within-galaxy shells are correlated (per-galaxy median index reported), the weighted KS treats elements as independent and the scale s is estimated (p-values approximate), inner baryon-dominated rings dropped (117 of 606 candidate shells), FIGGS carries no committed radii and MIGHTEE no masses (both flagged).  VERDICT: BOTH TESTS DEPART: measured dM/dg does not reproduce the kernel's -2 index or the full Lomax shape on the committed samples -- the measured mass-weighted acceleration distribution of the dark sector is NOT the Lomax the kernel predicts, as a composite (per-halo the -2 identity holds but is the flat-curve kinematic tautology and is statistically unconstrained)."
